What it is:

This utility reads the given disk device sequentially, compiling a list of
bad sectors. Upon stumbling on a bad sector, the utility assumes it is part of a
larger span of bad blocks (which is usually true for mechanical hard disk drives).
So it remembers the bad "span" and continues checking past its end, saving time.
It also dumps a picture with visual representation of the bad areas, and some
text files.
In particular, it dumps commands to create partitions that avoid any bad sectors
on the disk. That is, partitions are created in such a manner that bad sectors are
left between them.
Of course, creating partitions means you lose all existing data on the disk.

Note 1:
THIS IS NOT A RECOVERY TOOL!
If you have a faulty disk with valuable data, contact a firm that specializes in
data recovery. Attempt to recover data yourself may have disastrous results,
even using "reputable" recovery software.

Note 2:
The program does NOT use low-level disk operations. Instead, it reads from the
disk device using ordinary file operations. This worked for me, but may not work
for you.

Note 3:
The program does not modify the disk it checks. But you may wish to use commands
it dumps to a *.shell file that do.

Note 4:
Even if you are lucky and your disk seems to work after partitioning, do not
assume it will last long. If the disk is faulty, it is faulty. More bad sectors
may appear over time, or the disk may simply die. Restoring data from a tricky
set of pertitions is impractical, so do not keep anything valuable on such a
disk.


Compiling:

Prerequisites:
1. Linux box with C++ compiler and python 2.x.
2. Qt development tools, headers and libs.

How to build:
$ tar xzf showbadblocks.tgz
$ cd showbadblocks
$ qmake
$ make

How to use:
# run under root
$ ./showbadblocks -s 10 [-t 1500] /dev/sdX
... wait till it finishes ...
... it saves files every 10 minutes ...

Here, "-s 10" means spans are 10 megabytes.
"-t 1500" means we also consider slow-to-read sectors bad. Here, sectors that
take longer than 1500 milliseconds to read.

Files created:

badblocks_dev_sdX.png - map of bad blocks (picture). Red dots are actual bad
    sectors, grey dots are the entire spans we marked bad.
badblocks_dev_sdX.txt - list of bad blocks (not sorted!)
badblocks_dev_sdX.span - list of bad block spans.
badblocks_dev_sdX.shell - commands to create/use/delete partitions while
    avoiding bad sectors (use at your own risk).

